<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Package for the XrmToolBox Tool Library.

  Three rules the Tool Library enforces, and how this file meets them:

  1. The package version must match the tool assembly version, or the tool shows as
     permanently out of date. Both are set from <Version> in the csproj; keep them in step.
  2. The dependency must be on "XrmToolBox" itself at the minimum version you target, never on
     XrmToolBoxPackage.
  3. Every file must sit under a "Plugins" folder, and the package must contain only assemblies
     XrmToolBox does not already ship. This tool references the Dataverse SDK, WebView2 and
     Newtonsoft.Json, all of which come with XrmToolBox, so only our own assembly is packed.

  Build:
     dotnet build src\Oliver4.DataverseModelDesigner -c Release
     nuget pack package\Oliver4.DataverseModelDesigner.nuspec -Version 1.10.0
-->
<package xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/packaging/2013/05/nuspec.xsd">
  <metadata>
    <id>Oliver4.DataverseModelDesigner</id>
    <version>1.10.0</version>
    <title>Dataverse Model Designer</title>
    <authors>Oliver4</authors>
    <owners>Oliver4</owners>
    <license type="expression">MIT</license>
    <projectUrl>https://github.com/oliver4-devtools/dataverse-model-designer</projectUrl>
    <iconUrl>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oliver4-devtools/dataverse-model-designer/main/docs/icon.png</iconUrl>
    <requireLicenseAcceptance>false</requireLicenseAcceptance>
    <summary>Explore, design and document a Dataverse data model from inside XrmToolBox.</summary>
    <description>
      A data-model exploration and design tool, not just an ERD exporter.

      Build a focused diagram from a solution or a set of tables, guided step by step.
      Inspect a relationship's schema name, lookup column and full cascade configuration without
      leaving the canvas. Switch between tables-only, relationship-column and all-column detail.
      Add proposed, external and deprecated objects alongside the current model, visibly
      distinguished without relying on colour alone. Save an editable diagram, reopen it later and
      refresh it against Dataverse with a difference review.

      Exports to PNG, SVG, draw.io and Mermaid, and experimentally to Visio.

      The tool only ever reads metadata. Nothing it does changes the connected environment.
    </description>
    <releaseNotes>
      1.10.0 - changes from real-world use of the tool.

      Depth now means the whole model. An annotation sent behind the tables goes behind the
      relationship lines as well, which is where both picture exporters had always put it. Text
      boxes and arrows are drawn over sticky notes on the same side, so a label lying on a note
      stays readable.

      A selected sticky note has a rotation handle above its top edge. Drag it to set the angle -
      Shift for 15 degree steps, Ctrl for fine adjustment - or pick one from the new Angle box in
      the inspector, where Straight squares the note to the page. A note nobody has turned keeps
      the slight slant it has always been drawn with. draw.io carries a hand-set angle; Visio
      writes the shape square.

      Command bar: Auto-layout has moved to the end of the Draw group, "+ Add tables" is now "Add
      existing tables", "Propose" is "Propose new tables" and "Explore" is "Explore relationships".

      The legend sits underneath the panels, so dragging it no longer parks it on top of the table
      or relationship inspector.

      Fixed: connecting to an environment as the tool starts announced the connection twice.

      1.9.0 - changes from real-world use of the tool.

      A sticky note, a text box or an arrow can be drawn in front of the table cards or behind
      them, chosen per annotation in the inspector or from its right-click menu. In front is the
      default, and it is what an annotation in a diagram written before 1.9.0 becomes - everything
      used to be painted below the cards with no way to say otherwise. Behind means a card drawn
      over it hides it, and clicking there selects the card. Every export draws it on the same side.

      Sticky notes resize freely again, on each axis. A new one is still 140 x 140 with 14px bold
      text; that is now a starting size rather than a rule. Resizing moves in whole grid steps from
      wherever the annotation already is, so nudging the grip of a new note no longer jumps it off
      its own size.

      1.8.0 - changes from real-world use of the tool.

      The legend can be dragged anywhere on the canvas. Its position is saved with the diagram, and
      right-clicking it offers a way to put it back in the corner or to hide it. A position saved on
      a large screen is pulled back into view on a smaller one rather than being left off the edge.

      Sticky notes stay square when they are resized, and a new one is 140 x 140 with its text at
      14px bold. The size box in the inspector sets both sides of a note for the same reason. A
      note's own font size and weight now reach the draw.io and Visio exports, which were drawing
      every sticky note in the exporting application's default face.

      Proposing a relationship: the intended lookup column keeps the underscore after the publisher
      prefix, so a lookup to dev_project is offered as "dev_projectid" rather than "devprojectid" -
      the name goes onto the card, anchors the connector and reaches every export, so it was wrong
      in more than one place. The same correction applies to a column proposed by hand and to the
      default relationship schema name. With a table selected on the canvas, the dialog now starts
      from that table instead of from whichever table happens to be first in the diagram.

      Also fixed, found while making the above: an annotation in a hand-edited file with no size or
      font size on it was exported at three numbers the canvas has never drawn - it now exports at
      the size and text size the canvas shows. Where the legend has been dragged to is a screen
      position and cannot be carried into a picture, so the export dialog says so before you choose
      a filename.

      1.7.0 - changes from real-world use of the tool.

      New diagram: the tables step now ticks everything for you when a solution has fewer than 200
      tables, and says so when it has more. Next: Relationships asks you to confirm when more than
      20 tables are ticked, naming the number, so a solution's worth of tables cannot be added by
      clicking through. The first screen now carries the Oliver4 logo and the tool name.

      Table cards mark mandatory columns with a red asterisk - business required and system
      required alike.

      Sticky notes are tilted about twice as far, so the slant reads as deliberate. The box the
      marquee, Fit and the exported drawing use now allows for the corners a tilted note actually
      reaches.

      The resize handle on a sticky note or a text box only appears once the annotation is
      selected. It used to sit in the corner of every text box on the canvas, and dragging that
      corner reshaped a box nobody had selected.

      Proposing a relationship: Swap is now a button between the two table pickers rather than a
      line of text below them, and the intended lookup column is a required field that arrives
      pre-filled with the derived name. The connector for a proposed relationship now points at the
      lookup column at the many end and at the primary key at the one end, the way an existing
      relationship does. It used to give up and point at the middle of the card header whenever the
      one end had no primary key recorded yet, or had been swapped since the relationship was
      drawn.

      Exclude has gone from the relationship inspector and the canvas menu. It did exactly what
      Hide does - both kept the relationship in the file, off the canvas and out of every export -
      so there is now one control. An existing diagram opens with anything excluded shown as
      hidden, and the documentation still reports how many relationships are recorded but not
      drawn.

      The explorer's "Change settings" button has a row of its own above the filter instead of
      being squeezed in beside it.

      About and What this tool can do can no longer say the version is unavailable: if the version
      has not arrived yet they ask for it and fill it in.

      1.6.2 - a full test round of 1.6.1. Nothing here is a new feature; it is what two review
      passes over every module, and a verification pass over every finding, turned up.

      Refresh was reporting cardinality changes that had not happened. Dataverse reports each 1:N
      twice, once from each end, and the refresh kept whichever it read last - so a connector could
      be flipped to N:1 in the saved diagram, drawn backwards in draw.io, Visio and Mermaid, and
      then vanish from the Relationships panel with no way to bring it back. The two views are now
      merged rather than overwritten, which also stops a polymorphic lookup being reported as
      single-target.

      Refresh could not see anything that changed after the first read. Table metadata was cached
      for the life of the connection and nothing ever cleared it, so the second refresh in a
      session - and often the first - compared the diagram against a stale snapshot and said
      "Unchanged". It now re-reads the tables it is about to compare. A relationship created
      between two tables already on the diagram is reported too, where before only new columns were.

      Text holding a timestamp is no longer rewritten. A note, description or title whose whole
      value read as a date, such as 2026-06-30T09:00:00Z, came back reformatted as 06/30/2026
      09:00:00 - on opening the file and again on every save.

      Promoting a proposed relationship that the developer built the other way round no longer
      settles the lookup onto the wrong card, and can no longer mark a table's own primary key as
      the relationship's lookup.

      Multi-select choice columns, and file and image columns, are no longer dropped from every
      card, export and document.

      One diagram's private column notes, hidden columns and deprecated statuses no longer leak
      into the next diagram opened on the same connection.

      The canvas is faster. Measuring a card was spending 99.5% of its time building the key that
      was supposed to save the measurement, so dragging a card on a 200-table model ran at about
      one frame a second. It now runs at about sixty. The same change fixes a card that could draw
      another connector's lookup column after connectors were hidden and shown again.

      Exports: Visio pages are sized to the drawing instead of a fixed 16x11in, and a connector
      running down or to the left is no longer mirrored. A Mermaid diagram no longer loses a table
      that has no connector, and no longer fails to render when the diagram title contains a colon.
      Alternate keys are marked in exports as they are on the canvas. Column order in an export now
      matches the canvas on a non-English Windows. A proposed relationship excluded from the
      diagram no longer takes its lookup column out of the design register with it. The draw.io
      legend explains the emphasis colours it draws, and its preflight no longer describes
      limitations draw.io does not have.

      Keyboard and dialogs: Delete, Ctrl+A, Ctrl+Z and the zoom keys no longer act on the canvas
      behind an open dialog, or while you are typing in a field. Tick boxes and selectable rows in
      every dialog can be reached from the keyboard. Typing a note no longer pushes one undo entry
      per keystroke, which used to discard the whole undo history. Cancelling a path search no
      longer raises an error saying the designer broke. Light-theme small text now meets 4.5:1,
      which it did not; dark mode already did.

      Saving tells you where your work is if the write fails, and a very long folder path no
      longer fails with a message about the drive being full.

      1.6.1 - Fixes to the 1.6.0 work, found by testing it rather than by using it.

      A lookup column that a proposed relationship owns is now left alone by the controls that used
      to take it off the card: unticking it, and the None button beside the column list, are both
      refused with a message naming the relationship, and so is the deprecate clock - a column drawn
      struck through beside a connector drawn as live said two contradictory things about one object.

      Adding tables again no longer puts the same logical name on a card twice. A real column whose
      name a standing proposal already claims is held back, exactly as a refresh holds it back, and
      a real relationship that describes a standing proposal is not drawn beside it as a second
      connector. Nothing proposed is promoted without being confirmed, so the proposal is what
      stands until Refresh offers the match.

      Refresh: promoting a proposed relationship now takes the lookup column name the environment
      really uses and clears the invented one off the card, and the relationship's lookup is no
      longer offered as a second, independent tick box for the same design decision. Promoting a
      proposed table no longer lists its columns twice. The review dialog says what it is offering
      to promote, and its list of what a refresh preserves now names sticky notes, text boxes and
      arrows rather than just notes.

      Export: a canvas of text boxes and arrows with no table on it can be exported - Save has always
      treated it as a diagram. The Visio warnings tell arrows and notes apart instead of counting
      every drawing as a note.

      Documentation exports now list a table's columns in the order the canvas draws them - primary
      key, primary name, lookups, then the rest - and honour the Column order setting, which
      previously changed the picture and no document at all. Mermaid honours a collapsed card. The
      register lists a proposed relationship once rather than twice, with a line saying which lookup
      column it brings, and annotations are listed by kind with drawn arrows accounted for.

      1.6.0 - Relationships that create their own lookup, a crow's foot the right way round, and
      things you can draw on the canvas.

      Proposing a one-to-many relationship now writes the lookup column it implies onto the table at
      the many end, and the connector is drawn to that row rather than to the middle of the card
      header. The column belongs to the relationship: rename the lookup and it is renamed, change
      which table is at the many end and it moves, delete the relationship and it goes. Because of
      that, Lookup, Customer and Owner have been taken out of the column editors, which now say why
      and offer a button through to the relationship editor.

      The "many" terminator on a connector was drawn backwards - apex on the card, prongs out in the
      whitespace - so it read as an arrowhead pointing into the table rather than as a crow's foot.
      The three prongs now touch the card.

      New draw tools, grouped on the toolbar after Cascade. Add sticky note places a paper square,
      square-cornered and slightly tilted, rather than another rounded box. Text box places text with
      no background and no border, for labelling a region of the canvas. Arrow is dragged out
      anywhere and can be recoloured; hold Shift to keep it straight, and drag either end to re-aim it.

      Explore relationships is two screens instead of one. The settings take the whole dialog and
      Explore is the primary button in the footer, which cannot be scrolled away from - the old
      layout put it at the bottom of a narrow column where it was easy to miss entirely.

      Find path lists the paths that are entirely on the diagram first, flags them in green, and says
      that clicking one highlights it on the canvas. The ones that are not name the tables missing.

      Also: Close diagram in the menu; the grid and light/dark toggles are labelled buttons rather
      than bare glyphs, and Display is now Display settings; bigger, sharper artwork on the About box
      and the feature guide, with OLIVER4 above the tool name; and the version in the XrmToolBox tool
      list now reads 1.6.0 rather than 1.6.0.0.

      1.5.0 - A toolbar that fits, notes you can read on the canvas, and a dark mode you can read at all.

      The command bar now sheds one button at a time as the window narrows instead of dropping all
      six canvas actions at once, so a half-width tool pane shows Explore, Auto-layout and Propose
      rather than "+ Add tables" and a band of empty space. Whatever it does shed appears at the top
      of the overflow menu, which is now capped to the window height and scrolls - on a short window
      it used to measure taller than the screen and run its first items off the top edge, which was
      the one place the shed commands were supposed to be.

      Fixed: proposing a table raised "model.validate is not a function" and did nothing. The
      dialog's own validation was wired up one line after the dialog was built.

      Table cards. A proposed column now carries the same yellow tint a proposed table does, so it
      is findable on a card of twenty real ones and survives a black-and-white print. The NOTE tag
      is a button: click it and the note opens on the canvas next to the card, with a close cross.

      The table inspector's "Columns on this card" now means it. The ticks show what is actually
      drawn rather than what is merely permitted, and switching a table to "All selected columns"
      keeps the card it already had - so the next tick adds one column instead of forty.

      Legend. Rebuilt from the palette the canvas actually draws with, so a swatch looks like the
      card it stands for, dashed borders included. Emphasis colours applied by hand are listed too,
      and can be named: click one in the legend and call it "Phase 2" or "Owned by Finance", and
      that name appears in the legend and in exports. The names are saved in the diagram.

      Dark mode is higher contrast throughout - card text near-white instead of grey, panel edges
      that separate a floating panel from the canvas behind it, and stronger status tints.

      A long-running job can be cancelled. After a second and a half the progress card offers a
      Cancel button, which stops the work in the host rather than only hiding the overlay. The
      overlay itself now draws above dialogs: a metadata read started from inside Add tables or
      Explore used to run behind the dialog that started it, so nothing on screen said the tool was
      busy. Error and warning messages have a close cross.

      Also: new diagrams open at 100% rather than at whatever percentage happened to fit; a saved
      diagram reopens at the view it was saved with instead of being refitted; the toolbar's
      "Annotate" is now "Add note"; the About box and the feature guide carry the tool name, the
      version and the logo, in artwork drawn for the theme in use; and "Copyright" is now ©.

      1.4.0 - What a delete takes with it, a document you can hand over, and who owns what.

      Cascade impact. Pick a table and see what deleting or reassigning one of its records actually
      reaches. The relationship inspector has always shown one connector's cascade configuration;
      this follows the chain - Account cascades to Contact, Contact cascades on to something else -
      and answers in three parts: what is deleted or reassigned with it, what would refuse the
      operation outright (Restrict), and what keeps its records but loses the link (RemoveLink).
      The walk reads live metadata rather than the diagram, because a cascade into a table you did
      not think to draw is exactly the case that bites; tables in the answer that are not on the
      diagram are marked as such, and the chain can be highlighted on the canvas. Available from
      the toolbar and from a table's right-click menu.

      Documentation export. Two new formats produce the data-model section of a design document
      rather than a picture: Markdown ready for an Azure DevOps wiki, and a self-contained HTML
      page that prints. Both carry the table catalogue with ownership and columns, the relationship
      list with its full cascade behaviour, alternate keys, and a register of every proposed and
      deprecated object with the note explaining why - which is the part nothing else records. The
      Markdown embeds a generated Mermaid diagram. Columns follow the diagram's own detail
      settings, so the document says what the diagram says.

      Ownership overlay. Tables now carry how their records are owned - user or team, organisation,
      business unit - read from metadata, shown in the inspector, included in the documentation and
      available as a marker on each card from Display settings. It is a marker rather than a colour
      because the four status colours are already load-bearing. A change to ownership is reported
      by refresh, where it is one of the more consequential things a refresh can find.

      Notes: cascade analysis reads relationship metadata only - no record is read, and nothing is
      changed in the diagram or in Dataverse. Diagram files written by 1.3.0 open unchanged; the
      file format is still version 2, since ownership is an additive property.

      1.3.0 - Explore the model, and one place to propose a change.

      Explore relationships. Pick one table and see everything one relationship away from it; raise
      the depth and the walk goes another hop out from what it found, grouped by hop so the shape
      of the neighbourhood stays readable. Filters decide what is followed and what is left out -
      lookups in either direction, many-to-many, Microsoft-supplied tables, activity tables, N:N
      intersect tables, and the platform plumbing that every record relates to. The walk reads live
      metadata a table at a time and names the table it is reading while it works. Results are
      reviewed before anything is added, then used to create a diagram or added to the current one.

      Propose. A single button for every kind of design work: a new table, a column on a table that
      already exists, a relationship between any two tables, or an external system. A proposed
      relationship can now join two tables that both already exist in Dataverse - the commonest
      future-state change of all, and previously the one thing the tool could not express. A table
      card also offers "Draw a relationship to...", which arms the canvas so the next card clicked
      becomes the other end.

      Search now reaches past the diagram. Columns that match appear as results in their own right
      rather than only surfacing their table, and a table found elsewhere in the environment can be
      added straight from the results, with its relationships to what is already on the canvas.

      Also: an existing column can be marked deprecated; the relationship list filters by table;
      notes attach to connectors as well as cards; the diagram description shows on the canvas; and
      the model list marks hub and orphan tables.

      Refresh reports more. A column added in the environment since the last refresh is now listed
      as a change rather than merged in silently, along with relationship renames, cascade
      configuration that has been removed, alternate-key changes and several other updates that
      were previously applied without being mentioned.

      A "What this tool can do" guide is in the overflow menu.

      Fixed: adding tables to an existing diagram never looked for relationships between the new
      tables and the ones already on the canvas, which is the main thing "add to this diagram" is
      for. The path finder announced a highlight and drew nothing unless something happened to be
      selected. Reopening a proposed table's editor rewired every proposed relationship on it to
      the wrong table. The column schema-name switch had no effect in the default configuration.
      Turning off relationship type hid the label but left the crow's feet on every connector.
      Turning off automatic positioning stacked every new table on the origin. Auto and Horizontal
      layouts were the same algorithm under two names.

      Error handling has been worked through end to end. A lost or expired connection, a missing
      privilege, an unreachable environment, a file that has been moved, one that is open in
      another program, a read-only folder, a diagram from a newer version of the tool, a damaged
      file - each now says what happened and what to do about it. Saving writes to a temporary file
      and replaces the original in one step, so a failed save can never leave you with less than
      you started with.

      The file format is now version 2. Diagrams saved by earlier versions are upgraded when opened
      and you are told; a version 2 file cannot be opened by an earlier build. The unreachable DBML
      and JSON exports and several pieces of dead code have been removed.

      1.2.0 - Dark mode, editable proposals and a working path finder.
      A light and dark theme, toggled from the toolbar and remembered between sessions; exports
      stay light whichever theme is on screen. The command bar keeps the tool and diagram names
      readable at any window size and moves controls into its overflow menu as space runs out.
      Proposed columns can now be added to real tables and edited afterwards, and a proposed table
      can be reopened and changed. Notes are resized by dragging their bottom-right corner. The
      zoom readout opens a list of levels. Twelve emphasis colours instead of six, still applied
      to the card header and border only, and the inspector swatches now show the header tint the
      card will actually get.
      The canvas grid is more visible and has a toolbar toggle. Mandatory fields are marked with
      a red asterisk and example text has been removed from the proposed-table dialog, along with
      the paste-from-CSV option. Export has moved into the overflow menu.

      Fixed: the relationship path finder reported "no path found" for every search. A comparison
      against int.MaxValue + 1 overflowed to int.MinValue, which discarded every candidate path
      before it was explored. It now finds paths, does not re-read a table it has already walked,
      and says when the expansion budget stopped it early.

      A proposed column whose schema name now exists in Dataverse is offered for promotion in the
      refresh review, pre-ticked, on the same terms as a proposed table: nothing is promoted
      without confirmation. While the match is unconfirmed the real column is left off the card so
      it does not appear twice.

      The version number is now three-part (1.2.0) wherever it is shown.

      1.1.0 - Guided start.
      The diagram wizard now opens with the tool and asks one question at a time: starting point,
      then solution, then tables, then relationships. Zoom defaults to 100% and steps in 5%
      increments. The canvas pans with a right-click drag. Connector lines can be dragged clear of
      each other and hop over the lines they cross, and the table end of a connector now meets the
      middle of the card header. Emphasis colour tints the card header as well as its border.
      A table with a note carries a NOTE tag on the canvas. Column visibility now takes effect
      immediately. Display profiles, the connectivity summary, relationship-depth discovery and
      the DBML and JSON exports have been removed.

      1.0.0 - First release.
      Solution and catalogue diagram sources; relationship inclusion list and
      filters; tables-only, relationship-column and all-column detail modes with per-table
      overrides; interactive canvas with pan, zoom, drag, marquee selection, undo and five
      automatic layouts; relationship inspector with full cascade configuration; proposed,
      external and deprecated objects with a design editor; canvas notes; relationship path
      finder; save and reopen .dvmd diagrams; refresh with a difference review and confirmed
      promotion of proposed objects; export to PNG, SVG, draw.io, Mermaid and Visio
      (experimental), each with an explicit statement of what the format cannot carry.
    </releaseNotes>
    <copyright>© 2026 Oliver4</copyright>
    <tags>XrmToolBox Dataverse Dynamics365 PowerPlatform ERD DataModel Diagram Architecture Documentation</tags>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency id="XrmToolBox" version="1.2025.10.74" />
    </dependencies>
  </metadata>
  <files>
    <file src="..\src\Oliver4.DataverseModelDesigner\bin\Release\Oliver4.DataverseModelDesigner.dll"
          target="lib\net48\Plugins" />
  </files>
</package>
